WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.027
Go improves memory safety compared with
languages that require manual memory

2
00:00:04.107 --> 00:00:07.100
management, but secure programs still need
resource discipline.

3
00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.814
A service can be correct from a
memory-safety point of view and still fail because

4
00:00:10.894 --> 00:00:13.993
it allocates too much, reads too much,
opens too much, waits too long,

5
00:00:14.073 --> 00:00:16.051
or forgets to release something important.

6
00:00:16.131 --> 00:00:20.032
Slices, maps, buffers, and decoded objects
should have practical size expectations.

7
00:00:20.112 --> 00:00:21.608
Reading an entire request, file,

8
00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:25.215
or stream into memory may be fine for a
small administrative tool and risky for a

9
00:00:25.295 --> 00:00:26.043
public service.

10
00:00:26.123 --> 00:00:29.009
Bounded readers, request size limits,
pagination, streaming,

11
00:00:29.089 --> 00:00:32.082
and careful allocation patterns help keep
behavior predictable.

12
00:00:32.162 --> 00:00:34.406
Resource lifecycles deserve the same
attention.

13
00:00:34.486 --> 00:00:38.013
Files, network connections, response
bodies, database rows, locks, timers,

14
00:00:38.093 --> 00:00:40.658
and goroutines should have clear ownership
and cleanup.

15
00:00:40.738 --> 00:00:43.410
defer is useful because it keeps cleanup
near acquisition,

16
00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.628
but it should be used with lifecycle
awareness.

17
00:00:45.708 --> 00:00:49.075
A defer inside a long-running loop may
delay cleanup longer than intended.

18
00:00:49.155 --> 00:00:51.132
Timeouts and limits protect availability.

19
00:00:51.212 --> 00:00:53.884
HTTP clients, servers, database calls,
queue operations,

20
00:00:53.964 --> 00:00:56.636
and background workers should not wait
forever by default.

21
00:00:56.716 --> 00:01:00.190
When failure occurs, code should return to
a known state, release resources,

22
00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.942
and provide enough operational signal for
investigation.

23
00:01:03.022 --> 00:01:04.144
The unsafe package, cgo,

24
00:01:04.224 --> 00:01:07.110
and low-level runtime assumptions should
be rare and reviewed.

25
00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.595
There may be legitimate reasons in
specialized code,

26
00:01:09.675 --> 00:01:13.309
but ordinary application security is
stronger when teams stay within Go's safer

27
00:01:13.389 --> 00:01:15.633
runtime model and make resource limits
explicit.

28
00:01:15.713 --> 00:01:19.454
Resource decisions should also appear in
tests and runbooks so operators know what

29
00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:22.580
normal pressure looks like and what limits
are expected to protect.
